<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/advanced/psycholinguistics-and-acquisition">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Grammar, phonology and semantics described the system; psycholinguistics asks how that system
    <strong>runs in a human head and develops in one</strong>: how an infant extracts the grammar of a
    community from noisy, imperfect speech, how adults parse sentences millisecond by millisecond, and what
    bilingual minds reveal about the machinery. It is the field's meeting point with psychology and
    neuroscience, and it contains linguistics' fiercest live debate — how much of grammar is learned, how
    much is built in — argued with real data on both sides.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="acquisition, the innateness debate, real-time parsing, bilingual minds" widget="equationStep" config={psylingCfg} />

  <h2>How children crack the code: the timeline and the telltale errors</h2>
  <p>
    The trajectory is strikingly uniform across languages and cultures. Cooing by 3 months; canonical
    <strong>babbling</strong> (bababa) around 6–9 months; perceptual tuning in the first year — infants
    initially discriminate every phonetic contrast in every language, then, near their first birthday, lose
    the contrasts their language ignores (Werker and Tees) — the dialect-specific ear is <em>learned</em>.
    First words near 12 months; two-word combinations (telegraphic, word order already correct) around
    18–24 months; morphological and syntactic explosion through years 2–4. The methodological breakthrough
    was Jean Berko's <strong>wug test</strong> (1958): show a child a drawing of a nonsense creature —
    "this is a wug; now there are two of them; there are two ___" — and four-year-olds say <em>wugs</em>.
    They have never heard the word; they must possess a rule, not a memorised list.
  </p>
  <p>
    Even more telling is what children get <em>wrong</em>. They say "goed" and "foots" <em>after</em> a
    period of correctly using "went" and "feet" — the <strong>U-shaped learning curve</strong>. Imitation
    cannot produce that pattern (no adult says "goed"); only a rule ("add -ed") colliding with stored
    exceptions can. And the errors children <em>never</em> make are equally diagnostic: given "the boy who
    is crying is happy," no child extracts the question by simple linear movement ("is the boy who is crying
    is happy?") — Crain and Nakayama (1987) showed even 3–5-year-olds apply the structure-dependent rule
    (move the <em>main</em> clause's auxiliary), a fact that sits at the centre of the innateness debate,
    since no simple induction from the input teaches it.
  </p>

  <h2>Poverty of stimulus: the field's central argument</h2>
  <p>
    Chomsky's argument runs: the child's input is finite, partly ungrammatical, and information-poor; yet
    every child converges on a rich, specific grammar, rapidly, with startling uniformity, including
    knowledge (like structure-dependence and subtleties of binding and islands) that the input does not
    plausibly contain. Something about the learning machinery must therefore be <strong>innate and
    language-specific</strong> — universal grammar. The counter-case, now formidable, comes from
    <strong>usage-based and constructionist</strong> approaches (Tomasello): children learn item-specific
    constructions ("want X," "gonna V") and only gradually abstract; distributional statistics in the input
    are richer than intuitions suggest; and infants are staggering statistical learners — Saffran and
    colleagues (1996) showed 8-month-olds segment words from fluent speech using transitional probabilities
    after minutes of exposure. The modern position is genuinely unresolved: statistical learning is real and
    powerful, but whether general mechanisms suffice for all of syntax — with the developmental timing,
    the species specificity, and the creole evidence below still pointing the other way — is the field's
    open core.
  </p>

  <h2>Natural experiments: creoles, sign, and the critical period</h2>
  <p>
    History sometimes runs the experiment for us. <strong>Pidgins</strong> are rudimentary contact systems
    (no inflection, fixed word order); Bickerton documented how, in Hawaii and elsewhere, the
    <strong>children of pidgin speakers created creoles in one generation</strong> — full grammars with
    tense-aspect systems and embedded clauses, built from input that lacked them. In <strong>Nicaragua,
    deaf children who had each invented home gestures were brought together in new schools after 1979</strong>
    and collectively created Nicaraguan Sign Language; cohorts entering later children refined it — spatial
    grammar, verb agreement — emerging <em>across</em> the childhood community (Senghas's studies), grammar
    crystallising under children's hands. And <strong>sign languages acquired by deaf children of deaf
    parents develop on the same schedule as spoken language</strong> — babbling in the hands, the same
    milestones — establishing that the language faculty is modality-independent: it wants structured
    symbolic communication, in any channel.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The critical (better: sensitive) period</strong> completes the picture. Lenneberg (1967)
    proposed native-like acquisition requires exposure in childhood; the evidence is converging rather than
    a single study — deaf children denied early sign input (the tragic "linguistic deprivation" cases, of
    which Genie, found at 13 in 1970, is the most cited) show lasting syntactic deficits; large
    age-of-arrival studies in second language and late ASL learners show a smooth, monotonic decline in
    ultimate attainment with age of first exposure, steepest before roughly 7 and flattening after puberty.
    Late learners can be superbly fluent and accurate; what declines measurably is the ability to achieve
    fully native-like intuitions. Child language, in short, runs on a developmental window.
  </p>

  <h2>Parsing in real time: the millisecond machinery</h2>
  <p>
    Adult performance is its own laboratory. The comprehension system is <strong>incremental</strong>: it
    commits to a structure word by word rather than waiting for the end — which is why Bever's garden-path
    sentence "The horse raced past the barn fell" stops readers cold: "raced" is initially parsed as the
    main verb, and the later "fell" finds the sentence already structurally committed. Eye-tracking shows
    the parser is also <strong>predictive</strong> — hearing a verb licenses anticipatory looks at plausible
    objects (the "visual world" paradigm) — and probabilistic: referential context ("the horse that was
    raced..." setups) and even prosody override the bare preferences, so real comprehension integrates
    syntax, meaning, and world knowledge continuously (Trueswell and colleagues). Event-related potentials
    timestamp the stages: the <strong>N400</strong> peaks at semantic anomalies ("I take my coffee with
    cream and <em>socks</em>"), the <strong>P600</strong> at syntactic violations and garden-path
    reanalysis — two distinct brain responses a few hundred milliseconds apart, revealing morphology, meaning and
    structure as separately trackable streams. Production shows the same architecture mirrored: speech
    errors respect linguistic units (whole words swap with words, phonemes with phonemes), slips of the
    tongue confirm the levels of the grammar, and the tip-of-the-tongue state isolates retrieval of a known
    word's meaning from its sound.
  </p>

  <h2>Bilingual minds</h2>
  <p>
    The bilingual brain retired the last folk myth (two languages confusing a child — bilingual children
    meet milestones on schedule, sometimes with vocabulary distributed across languages). The cognitive
    findings are subtler: <strong>both languages are co-active</strong> even in single-language use
    (cross-language priming, eye-tracking on interlingual homophones), so managing them is a continuous
    attentional task; switching carries measurable costs (milliseconds, in switch trials) yet fluent
    code-switching as observed in sociolinguistics is grammatically disciplined, not random. The
    controversial claim — that lifelong bilingualism enhances domain-general executive function (the
    "bilingual advantage," Bialystok) — has become the field's public replication battleground: Paap's
    failed replications and publication-bias analyses have cut the effect down, and the honest current
    summary is that far-reaching cognitive benefits are unproven while co-activation and its management
    are certain.
  </p>

  <h2>The bilingual child, and the reading mind</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bilingual first language acquisition</strong> shows the system's modularity cleanly. Children
    raised with two languages from birth differentiate them early (by roughly age 2, adjusting language
    choice to interlocutor), meet monolingual milestones on schedule with vocabulary <em>distributed</em>
    across the two lexicons (knowing "dog" in one language and "cat" in the other is normal, not deficit),
    and produce <strong>intra-sentential mixing that obeys both grammars</strong> — a Spanish–English
    toddler places the English noun inside the Spanish phrase frame, not randomly. The developmental
    fear ("two languages will confuse the child") has no empirical support; what the data show is
    context-sensitive, rule-governed differentiation — the psycholinguistic foundation under the
    sociolinguistics lesson's code-switching. A second system worth understanding is the one the school
    layer adds: <strong>literacy</strong>. Reading must be taught and is built on
    <strong>phonological awareness</strong> — the discovery that speech decomposes into phonemes, which
    letters represent — and phoneme-awareness training is the best-established predictor-intervention in
    education; comprehension, meanwhile, draws on vocabulary and background knowledge, which is why a
    child can decode fluently and still understand little, and why knowledge-building curricula are a
    live policy debate. Spoken language is species-typical and takes care of itself; written language is
    a cultural technology riding on it — the clearest illustration in all of psycholinguistics of the
    nature/culture split within one mind.
  </p>

  <h2>The production side, worked: from thought to sound</h2>
  <p>
    Comprehension gets the headlines, but production is where the architecture shows its seams. Levelt's
    classic model stages the work: a <strong>conceptualiser</strong> turns intention into a preverbal
    message; a <strong>formulator</strong> retrieves words (lemmas — the meaning-plus-syntax entries) and
    builds phonological form, then articulation executes; a monitor listens to your own speech (and even
    inner speech) for errors. The evidence is the error pattern. <strong>Tip-of-the-tongue</strong> states
    split the stages: you know the meaning ("it's the thing on a ship's mast...") and often the first
    sound or syllable count, but not the word — lemma retrieved, phonology missing.
    <strong>Speech errors respect levels</strong>: meaning swaps ("write mother" for "letter... "
    word-exchanges at the lemma level), sound swaps ("tear dits" for "deer tits") at the phonological
    level, and — the classic — "you have hissed my mystery lectures" stranding s across words, showing
    planning chunks larger than the word. Errors are also <em>lawful</em>: exchanges swap same-category
    items, shifts move sounds to matching positions — production, like comprehension, is rule-governed
    machinery, and its slips are the machine's blueprints. The practical dividend: fluency in a second
    language is precisely the automatization of this pipeline (formulation without conscious monitoring),
    which is why the languages ladder's shadowing and chunk work target it directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three research programmes take you to the literature. <strong>Computational modelling</strong> now
    adjudicates the nurture-nature dispute with process models: connectionist networks trained on
    child-directed speech produce U-shaped overregularisation and wug-like generalisation without any
    dedicated grammar module — evidence the constructionists cite — while Bayesian and symbolic models of
    poverty-of-stimulus cases (auxiliary inversion, anaphoric one) argue the input underdetermines the
    grammar: both sides now argue with running code, which is progress. <strong>Genetics joins the
    conversation</strong>: the KE family's inherited speech-language disorder traced to a <em>FOXP2</em>
    mutation (2001) gave the first molecular handle on a human language capacity — with the essential
    caveat that FOXP2 is a transcription factor affecting motor sequencing broadly, not "a grammar gene"
    (the early headlines overreached; the gene's evolutionary history, including its Neanderthal identity
    to ours, is a live research area). <strong>The animal contrast</strong> keeps defining the target:
    Hockett's design features (productivity, displacement, duality) remain the checklist; trained apes
    learn symbol use and combinations but no convincing recursion or open-ended productivity; birdsong and
    whale song have learned structure without evident meaning; the search for continuity or discontinuity
    with human syntax is active and unsettled.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open questions at the frontier: does the critical period reflect neural maturation (the maturational
    account) or simply the opportunity structure of childhood (less entrenchment, more input, more need) —
    the two predict different interventions and the data are not yet decisive? Can statistical-learning
    accounts scale from word segmentation to full syntax? And the newest: do large language models, which
    acquire grammar from text alone, constitute a proof of concept for poverty-of-stimulus dissidents or
    for nativists — a question the field is actively fighting over, with input statistics now millionfold
    beyond any child's.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Berko (1958); Chomsky, <em>Aspects of the Theory of Syntax</em> (1965); Lenneberg (1967); Bickerton, <em>Roots of Language</em> (1981); Crain &amp; Nakayama (1987); Saffran, Aslin &amp; Newport, <em>Science</em> (1996); Senghas on Nicaraguan Sign Language; Lai et al. (2001) on FOXP2; Paap &amp; Greenberg (2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
